“its.” (Calmet) — Both sea and land may be in confusion; we shall fear nothing, having God for our protector. (Haydock) — Though many and noble personages have revolted from the faith in England, yet the Catholic Church will never fail, (Worthington) even if it should in these [British] islands. (Haydock)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Steam. Jerusalem was surrounded by placid streams, which are here opposed to the great waters, as in Isaias viii. 6. The Church, after persecution, is restored to peace, and adorned with all graces. (Calmet) — Tabernacle. This is the source of our joy, Apocalypse xxi., (Menochius) and xxii. 1., and Ezechiel xlvii. 1. — The advantages of the virtuous, both here and in heaven, are great. (Berthier)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Trembled. We have witnessed the commotions in the East, under Cyrus, and his son; the latter of whom seems to be styled Gog, (Ezechiel xxxviii. 19.) and perished in Judea, which he intended to plunder. (Calmet)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Shields. Hebrew, “the round things,” which some explain, “chariots,” without need. (Berthier) — Fire. The Fathers apply this to the peace which reigned at the birth of Christ, or to that which Constantine gave to the Church. (Calmet)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Still. We have only to admire the work of God. (Berthier) — He will bring all to a happy issue for his elect, though the wicked may rage. (Haydock)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}